==Norwegian Bokmål==

===Etymology 1===
Inflected form of {{term|vis|lang=nb}}.

====Adjective====
{{head|nb|adjective form}}

# {{form of|definite singular|vis|lang=nb}}
# {{plural of|vis|lang=nb}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|non|nb}} {{term|vísa||a manner of singing|lang=non}}, related to {{etyl|non|nb}} {{term|vis|lang=non}}.

====Noun====
{{nb-noun-c|vis}}

# [[lyrical|Lyrical]] or [[epic]] [[poem]] with plain content and plain [[melody]].
#* ''En strofisk dikt med strofisk melodi såväl litterärt som musikalisk oftast präglad av en '''viss''' enkelhet i stilen''. Bengt R. Jonsson (Swedish)
#*: A [[stanzaic]] poem with a stanzaic melody both literary and musically ususally characterized by to some degree a simplicity of style.

=====Usage notes=====
The Scandinavian term {{term|vise|lang=nb}} is less comprehensive than the English {{term|song|lang=en}} (Norwegian Bokmål {{term|sang|lang=nb}}), the German {{term|Lied|lang=de}} or the French {{term|chanson|lang=fr}}. The term {{term|sang}} is often citing a collective whereas the {{term|vise}} more commonly refers to an {{term|I}}.

=====Derived terms=====
* [[folkevise]]
* [[skillingsvise]]

=====Related terms=====
* [[sang#Norwegian Bokmål|sang]]

